STRATA

Semantic Stratigraphy Translation & Reconstruction Architecture

Repo status: runnable reference implementation
Purpose: Preserve what ordinary translation tends to flatten: ambiguity, morphology, rhetoric, sound, etymological echoes, historical strata, textual variants, cultural interfaces, and alternative readings.

> **Seal:** Translate the sentence; preserve the possibility-space.

────────

0. Problem

Translation is often treated as:

```text
SOURCE TEXT -> TARGET TEXT
```

But a historical text is not only a sequence of dictionary meanings. It may encode meaning through:

• morphology and grammatical choice,
• polysemy,
• idiom,
• word order,
• sound and rhythm,
• root/lemma echoes,
• rhetorical pattern,
• intertext,
• historical semantic layers,
• genre conventions,
• scribal/manuscript variation,
• deliberate ambiguity,
• culturally assumed knowledge,
• and relations that have no one-to-one English equivalent.

A single polished English line therefore frequently performs lossy compression.

STRATA changes the target.

```text
SOURCE
  -> evidence-preserving analysis
  -> semantic possibility lattice
  -> translation candidates
  -> annotations / footnotes / confidence
  -> reader-selectable rendering
```

The canonical output is not one translation.

It is a translation plus the structure English could not carry by itself.

────────

1. Canonical Principle

Let a source expression x expose a set of supported readings:

```text
R(x) = {r1, r2, ... rn}
```

A conventional translation often selects:

```text
T(x) = r_best
```

STRATA instead preserves:

```text
T*(x) = {
    surface_translation,
    literal_scaffold,
    viable_readings[],
    morphology[],
    rhetorical_features[],
    sound_features[],
    semantic_strata[],
    textual_variants[],
    cultural_context[],
    intertexts[],
    uncertainty,
    provenance[]
}
```

The goal is not maximal annotation.

The goal is minimum-loss transfer.

────────

2. The Translation Conservation Laws

STRATA attempts to conserve, where evidence permits:

1. Referential invariant — what entities/actions/relations are being discussed?
2. Relational invariant — how are they connected?
3. Ambiguity invariant — where the source leaves multiple live readings, do not silently kill them.
4. Register invariant — legal, poetic, vulgar, royal, intimate, liturgical, technical, etc.
5. Rhetorical invariant — parallelism, chiasm, repetition, pun, irony, intensification, omission.
6. Temporal-semantic invariant — what the expression plausibly meant in its own period, not only what descendants mean now.
7. Acoustic invariant — rhyme, alliteration, assonance, rhythm, phonetic resemblance where functionally meaningful.
8. Morphological invariant — information carried by inflection, root-pattern morphology, aspect, gender, number, case, state, etc.
9. Uncertainty invariant — uncertainty in the evidence must survive into the output.
10. Interface invariant — preserve how the original invited a reader/hearer to interact with it when reconstructable.

Not every invariant survives every language boundary.
When an invariant cannot be carried in the English line, move it into a note rather than erase it.

────────

3. Evidence Channels

Every annotation has a provenance class:

```text
OBSERVED       directly present in text/manuscript/corpus
ATTESTED       supported by lexicon, grammar, parallel usage, manuscript evidence
INFERRED       scholarly reconstruction from evidence
INTERPRETIVE   plausible reading dependent on interpretive choices
SPECULATIVE    interesting but weakly supported
COUNTERFACTUAL exploratory alternative, not evidence
```

Rule: confidence may never exceed evidence.

STRATA must never market an attractive pun, folk etymology, theological interpretation,
or modern word resemblance as an established feature of the ancient text.

────────

4. Semantic Stratigraphy Layer

For each significant lexical item:

```text
FORM(now-reading)
    ↓
LEMMA
    ↓
SENSES IN CORPUS/PERIOD
    ↓
EARLIER / NEIGHBORING SENSES
    ↓
ROOT / DERIVATIONAL FAMILY
    ↓
PARALLEL USES
    ↓
SEMANTIC DELTAS
```

The engine asks:

> What possibilities were available to a competent hearer at the time?

not:

> What clever association can a modern reader invent?

That distinction is the firewall between semantic stratigraphy and accidental mythology.

────────

5. The Easter-Egg Detector

An “Easter egg” is not automatically a secret intentional code.

STRATA uses the neutral term Layer Candidate.

A Layer Candidate may be:

```text
POLYSEMY
ROOT_ECHO
SOUND_PLAY
PARONOMASIA
ALLITERATION
ASSONANCE
RHYTHM
WORD_ORDER
REPETITION
PARALLELISM
CHIASM
IDIOM
ETYMOGRAPHIC_ECHO
INTERTEXT
GENRE_SIGNAL
CULTURAL_SCRIPT
GRAMMATICAL_AMBIGUITY
TEXTUAL_VARIANT
SEMANTIC_SHIFT
```

Each candidate receives:

```text
{
  "feature": "...",
  "evidence": ["..."],
  "intentionality": "unknown|unlikely|possible|probable|strong",
  "confidence": 0.0-1.0,
  "translation_loss_if_omitted": 0.0-1.0
}
```

Important: evidence for the feature and evidence that the author deliberately intended
the feature are separate questions.

