11. Translation Modes

```text
LITERAL       maximize structural correspondence
LITERARY      maximize equivalent artistic force
AMBIGUOUS     preserve multiple live readings
ACOUSTIC      prioritize sound/rhythm compensation
HISTORICAL    prioritize period-appropriate semantic range
INTERLINEAR   expose token-level mapping
STUDY         readable line + maximum useful annotation
POETIC        allow controlled target-language recreation
```

A translation should identify its mode.

There is no universal “most accurate” translation independent of the invariant being preserved.

────────

12. Sacred-Text Mode

Sacred texts deserve stricter epistemic separation because tiny translation choices may carry
large theological consequences.

Enable:

```text
sacred_text_mode = true
```

This requires:

1. Witness/edition always displayed.
2. Textual variants separated from translation choices.
3. Sectarian/theological interpretations labeled as interpretations.
4. Multiple historically important readings retained when supported.
5. No claim of recovering an author’s hidden intention without evidence.
6. No conflation of original composition language with later sacred translation traditions.
7. Notes distinguish:
  • linguistic possibility,
  • historical interpretation,
  • theological doctrine.
8. High-consequence claims require explicit sources.

This makes the engine useful across the Bible, Qur’an, Syriac literature,
rabbinic texts, classical works, poetry, law, inscriptions, and other historically layered texts
without pretending all traditions use identical textual histories.

